
### 🔍 Analysis of Script 9: What Stood Out & Why It Works

#### 1. Categorical Entry Regime & Exit Architecture Search

Unlike standard backtesters that only adjust period lengths (e.g., $10$ to $50$), Script 9 includes categorical strategy models as hyperparameters:

* **Entry Regimes (`entry_f`):**
* `0`: Unfiltered (Triggers on any short/long MA crossover).
* `1`: Momentum ($Close > Super\_MA$, buying strength).
* `2`: Value/Pullback ($Close < Super\_MA$, buying dips in uptrends).


* **Normal Stock Exit Models (`n_exit_m`):**
* `0`: Standard MA Crossunder ($MA_{\text{short}} < MA_{\text{long}}$).
* `1`: Percentage Trailing Stop ($Close < Peak \times (1 - \text{trail\_pct})$).
* `2`: Volatility ATR Trailing Stop ($Close < Peak - (\text{atr\_mult} \times ATR)$).



#### 2. Real-Time Sequential SIP Deployment

Script 9 tracks monthly $8,000\text{ INR}$ capital injections (`monthly_allowance`) alongside cash released from closed trades (`accumulated_capital`). Entries deploy in discrete $8,000\text{ INR}$ tickets. When multiple watchlist setups occur on the same bar, candidate assets are ranked dynamically using `wl_rank`:

* `0`: Deepest discount from signal price.
* `1`: Closest distance to long-term MA support.
* `2`: Highest velocity/momentum above short MA.

#### 3. High-Dividend Name Protection (`DIVIDEND_KINGS`)

Assets classified in `DIVIDEND_KINGS` (e.g., *ITC*, *Coal India*, *ONGC*, *PowerGrid*) use separate, non-destructive exit rules (`div_exit_m`). They ignore routine MA crossunders and exit only on deep peak drawdowns, Super MA violations, or prolonged time decay below the long MA.